// A `Self` in a trait-impl method's signature that SURVIVED the parser's fold is
// refused - and the position is the contract, not just the words. The reference
// resolves an impl's signatures while checking conformance, with the IMPL
// BLOCK's position, so the refusal lands on the `impl` line and not on the `fn`.
//
// The block's brace sits on its own line so the two positions differ by more than
// a column: a fixture where they share a line would pass whichever side is wrong.
trait Maker {
    fn make() -> Self;
}
struct P { x: int }
impl Maker for P
{
    fn make() -> Self {
        return P { x: 1 };
    }
}
fn main() { println(1); }
